+    <span class="keyword">return</span> reading * count</code></pre></figure><section id="part-1"><h2>Understand source, not backend machinery</h2><p>VOID’s diagnostic direction is clear .void locations, useful ranges and explanations that refer to language concepts. Normal errors should not require understanding generated LLVM IR, mangled symbols or linker internals.</p></section><section id="part-2"><h2>One language service</h2><p>The shared language server uses compiler-owned analysis for diagnostics and navigation. Implemented queries include completion, hover, signature help, symbols and guarded editing operations across editor integrations. These capabilities are still in development, with no public editor builds distributed here.</p></section><section id="part-3"><h2>Edits need semantic boundaries</h2><p>Rename and formatting deliberately operate on supported cases. Rename is not a universal operation on every declaration kind. Formatting is conservative rather than a full reflow or import-sorting system. Semantic highlighting and general code actions are not promised by the current server.</p></section><section id="part-4"><h2>The experience being built</h2><p>The goal is a useful everyday editor: quick answers about types and parameters, navigation that follows actual declarations, and feedback that stays attached to current source. Package discovery belongs to the future ecosystem; a native package manager is not available.</p></section><section id="current-tooling"><h2>Source-aware assistance, shared across editors</h2><p>Completion, documentation hover, parameter help, definitions, references, document/workspace symbols, guarded rename and conservative formatting are implemented through compiler-owned analysis. Module completion follows the selected source graph, including nested import paths. <code>##</code> documentation comments and <code>## @param</code> descriptions supply authored help. References inside string interpolation retain their source lo