-        Err(<span class="keyword">var</span> message): <span class="number">0</span></code></pre><div class="guide-result" data-source="/docs/snippets/errors.void" data-input="4" data-expected="6">Input <strong>4</strong><span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>Expected result <strong>6</strong></div></figure><section id="part-1"><h2>Return a recoverable outcome</h2><p><code>average</code> divides a total by a reading count. <code>result&lt;i32, string&gt;</code> carries either an integer average via <code>Ok</code> or an explanatory error via <code>Err</code>. Counts of zero or less are rejected before division.</p></section><section id="part-2"><h2>Handle both cases</h2><p>The match captures success with <code>Ok(var mean)</code>. For four readings totaling <code>24</code>, the result is <code>6</code>. This tiny CLI adapter deliberately uses zero as the application fallback when no valid average can be computed; a user-facing application would decide how to present <code>message</code>. This fallback is a policy of the example, not part of the result type.</p></section><section id="part-3"><h2>Propagate when appropriate</h2><p>In a compatible result-returning function, <code>?</code> unwraps success or propagates the exact matching error type. Recoverable errors remain distinct from runtime failures, such as invalid indexing or a failed checked conversion.</p></section><nav class="guide-pager" aria-label="Guide navigation"><a href="/docs/structs/">← Structs & memory</a><a href="/examples/">More examples</a></nav></article><nav class="guide-toc" aria-label="On this page"><span>ON THIS PAGE</span><a href="#part-1">Return a recoverable outcome</a><a href="#part-2">Handle both cases</a><a href="#part-3">Propagate when appropriate</a></nav></div></main><footer><div class="container footer-inner"><a class="brand" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 44 44" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M2 5h12l13 23-6 11z"/><path fill="#6b779b" d="M17 5h11l-6 10z"/><path fill="#303b