-<main id="main" class="inner-page"><div class="container guide-layout"><aside class="guide-sidebar"><nav aria-label="Documentation topics"><a class="guide-overview" href="/docs/">Documentation</a><details class="topic-group"><summary>Foundations</summary><a href="/docs/syntax/">Syntax basics</a><a href="/docs/variables/">Variables & constants</a><a href="/docs/types/">Types & numbers</a><a href="/docs/functions/">Functions</a><a href="/docs/control-flow/">Control flow</a><a href="/docs/modules/">Modules & imports</a></details><details class="topic-group"><summary>Data & behavior</summary><a href="/docs/collections/">Collections</a><a href="/docs/dictionaries/">Dictionaries</a><a href="/docs/strings/">Strings & Unicode</a><a href="/docs/structs/">Structs & memory</a><a href="/docs/initialization/">Initialization</a><a href="/docs/methods/">Receiver methods</a><a href="/docs/composition/">Composition</a><a href="/docs/inheritance/">Inheritance & dispatch</a><a href="/docs/interfaces/">Interfaces</a><a href="/docs/generics/">Generics</a><a href="/docs/properties/">Properties</a><a href="/docs/callables/">Callable structs</a><a href="/docs/closures/">Functions & closures</a><a href="/docs/events/">Events</a><a href="/docs/nullable/">Nullable values</a><a href="/docs/patterns/">Enums & pattern matching</a><a href="/docs/errors/">Results & errors</a><a href="/docs/cleanup/">Defer & resource cleanup</a></details><details class="topic-group"><summary>Concurrency</summary><a href="/docs/async/">Async & await</a><a href="/docs/task-groups/">Task groups & cancellation</a><a href="/docs/parallelism/">Parallel workers</a></details><details class="topic-group" open><summary>Tooling & integration</summary><a href="/docs/native-integration/">Native integration</a><a href="/docs/editor-tooling/">Editor tooling & diagnostics</a><a href="/docs/live-development/" aria-current="page">Live development</a></details></nav></aside><article class="guide-article"><p class="eyebrow">LEARN VOID / TOOLING &amp; INTEGRATION</p><h1 class="page-title">Live development</h1><p class="guide-tagline">Keep a running idea alive while its implementation changes.</p><p>Illustrative function fragment. A compatible replacement could change <code>steps * 2</code> to <code>steps * 3</code> while preserving this signature. This is a source-level illustration of the compatibility boundary, not an automatically watching command or proof of a live replacement session.</p><figure class="guide-code"><figcaption>live-development.void<a href="/docs/snippets/live-development.void" download>Download fragment</a></figcaption><pre tabindex="0" aria-label="live-development.void source"><code data-source="/docs/snippets/live-development.void"><span class="keyword">func</span> move_distance(steps: <span class="keyword">i32</span>) -&gt; <span class="keyword">i32</span>:
-    <span class="keyword">return</span> steps * <span class="number">2</span></code></pre></figure><section id="part-1"><h2>Why continuity matters</h2><p>For interactive applications, restarting can mean rebuilding the scene, reproducing an input sequence or finding the state you were investigating. VOID’s live-development goal is to apply compatible function-body changes while keeping the running program under the developer’s control.</p></section><section id="part-2"><h2>A compatibility boundary</h2><p>Runtime integration already supports compatible function replacement and keeps old code alive while in-flight work still needs it. Changes to type layouts, function signatures or incompatible capture layouts cannot be treated as a simple body replacement.</p></section><section id="part-3"><h2>What is planned</h2><p>Automatic file watching and save-triggered replacement remain future work. The current runtime integration is not an automatically watching development command. Incompatible changes require a deliberate restart rather than a forced restart that silently discards a session.</p></section><section id="part-4"><h2>Useful speed, measured</h2><p>A delivered debug-cache subset can reuse compatible compiled artifacts after source validation. This is separate from live replacement and does not serialize live application state. Performance work should be judged through real edit/build/run workloads, not an invented timing claim.</p></section><nav class="guide-pager" aria-label="Guide navigation"><a href="/docs/">← All guides</a><a href="/why-void/">Why VOID</a></nav></article><nav class="guide-toc" aria-label="On this page"><span>ON THIS PAGE</span><a href="#part-1">Why continuity matters</a><a href="#part-2">A compatibility boundary</a><a href="#part-3">What is planned</a><a href="#part-4">Useful speed, measured</a></nav></div></main><footer><div class="container footer-inner"><a class="brand" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 44 44" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M2 5h12l13 23-6 11z"/><path fill="#6b779b" d="M17 5h11l-6 10z"/><path fill="#303b64" d="M31 5h11L29 28l-6-10z"/></svg><span>VOID <small>LANGUAGE</small></span></a><p>Native code. Clear intent.</p><div><a href="/docs/">Documentation</a><a href="https://github.com/Shmellyorc/Void.Language">GitHub ↗</a></div></div></footer><div class="sr-only" id="announcement" role="status"></div></body></html>
