+    <span class="keyword">return</span> <span class="number">4</span></code></pre></figure><p><code>use io/memory as memory</code> imports the standard source <code>io/memory.void</code>. The alias supplies the name in the importing module. An absolute <code>VOID_STDLIB_ROOT</code> selects a standard-source root. Project-local library sources use <code>use lib/...</code>. Standard sources cannot import quoted project files. See <a href="/docs/binary-memory/">Binary values and memory streams</a>.</p></section><section id="entry-discovery"><h2>Find the project entry deliberately</h2><p>With a compatible local compiler, <code>voidc run .</code> discovers <code>main.void</code>. Directory-based <code>check</code> and <code>emit</code> use the same discovery. From an active file, discovery looks for the nearest exact <code>main.void</code> through parent directories up to the workspace boundary. Without an active file, only the workspace root is checked; there is no recursive search. An explicitly selected entry wins, source errors never trigger a fallback, and the selected entry remains fixed during a session until deliberately changed or restarted.</p><p>The complete source above uses the legacy <code>entry(i32)</code> adapter: run it with a numeric input. See <a href="/docs/syntax/#application">application entry</a> for a main-based program. These workflows describe implemented compiler behavior, not a public download or installation command.</p></section><nav class="guide-pager" aria-label="Guide navigation"><a href="/docs/">← All guides</a><a href="/examples/#modules">Explore examples</a></nav></article><nav class="guide-toc" aria-label="On this page"><span>ON THIS PAGE</span><a href="#part-1">Keep the module paths together</a><a href="#part-2">Use qualified and direct names</a><a href="#part-3">Read paths in project context</a><a href="#nested-imports">Follow paths from the importing file</a><a href="#entry-discovery">Find the project entry deliberately</a></nav><