-<main id="main" class="inner-page"><div class="container guide-layout"><aside class="guide-sidebar"><nav aria-label="Documentation topics"><a class="guide-overview" href="/docs/">Documentation</a><details class="topic-group"><summary>Foundations</summary><a href="/docs/syntax/">Syntax basics</a><a href="/docs/variables/">Variables & constants</a><a href="/docs/types/">Types & numbers</a><a href="/docs/functions/">Functions</a><a href="/docs/control-flow/">Control flow</a><a href="/docs/modules/">Modules & imports</a></details><details class="topic-group"><summary>Data & behavior</summary><a href="/docs/collections/">Collections</a><a href="/docs/dictionaries/">Dictionaries</a><a href="/docs/strings/">Strings & Unicode</a><a href="/docs/structs/">Structs & memory</a><a href="/docs/initialization/">Initialization</a><a href="/docs/methods/">Receiver methods</a><a href="/docs/composition/">Composition</a><a href="/docs/inheritance/">Inheritance & dispatch</a><a href="/docs/interfaces/">Interfaces</a><a href="/docs/generics/">Generics</a><a href="/docs/properties/">Properties</a><a href="/docs/callables/">Callable structs</a><a href="/docs/closures/">Functions & closures</a><a href="/docs/events/">Events</a><a href="/docs/nullable/">Nullable values</a><a href="/docs/patterns/">Enums & pattern matching</a><a href="/docs/errors/">Results & errors</a><a href="/docs/cleanup/">Defer & resource cleanup</a></details><details class="topic-group"><summary>Concurrency</summary><a href="/docs/async/">Async & await</a><a href="/docs/task-groups/">Task groups & cancellation</a><a href="/docs/parallelism/">Parallel workers</a></details><details class="topic-group" open><summary>Tooling & integration</summary><a href="/docs/native-integration/" aria-current="page">Native integration</a><a href="/docs/editor-tooling/">Editor tooling & diagnostics</a><a href="/docs/live-development/">Live development</a></details></nav></aside><article class="guide-article"><p class="eyebrow">LEARN VOID / TOOLING &amp; INTEGRATION</p><h1 class="page-title">Native integration</h1><p class="guide-tagline">Bring existing libraries into the language through deliberate boundaries.</p><p>No standalone native-binding program is offered here: the supported subset requires an explicitly trusted embedding host and its supplied virtual module. Ordinary CLI execution does not authorize arbitrary C libraries. The guide below explains that boundary without presenting proposed binding syntax as runnable code.</p><section id="part-1"><h2>The goal: native libraries that feel like VOID</h2><p>The long-term direction is typed .void wrappers around C libraries, keeping raw pointers, buffer ownership and foreign string representations out of ordinary application code. The compiler and runtime should carry the difficult boundary rules.</p></section><section id="part-2"><h2>What works today</h2><p>The implemented subset admits explicitly trusted, statically linked providers supplied by an embedding host. Two provider catalogs are available in the implementation, with one explicitly admitted descriptor per source graph. A descriptor supplies its virtual module; ordinary wrappers use managed resource identities, exact-width scalar types, owned UTF-8 transfers and rooted callbacks.</p></section><section id="part-3"><h2>What is still ahead</h2><p>This is not general-purpose native loading. There is no public arbitrary-library declaration syntax, dynamic loader or package resolver. Ordinary CLI and editor sessions do not automatically authorize native provider imports. Resource ownership, callback lifetime and thread restrictions remain part of the binding contract.</p></section><section id="part-4"><h2>Resources still need a lifetime</h2><p>Garbage collection does not close a native resource. The <a href="/docs/cleanup/">scoped cleanup guide</a> explains deterministic ownership. Wrappers use explicit release or deterministic deinit/with cleanup. Native resource handles cannot be transferred to worker tasks.</p></section><nav class="guide-pager" aria-label="Guide navigation"><a href="/docs/">← All guides</a><a href="/why-void/">Why VOID</a></nav></article><nav class="guide-toc" aria-label="On this page"><span>ON THIS PAGE</span><a href="#part-1">The goal: native libraries that feel like VOID</a><a href="#part-2">What works today</a><a href="#part-3">What is still ahead</a><a href="#part-4">Resources still need a lifetime</a></nav></div></main><footer><div class="container footer-inner"><a class="brand" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 44 44" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M2 5h12l13 23-6 11z"/><path fill="#6b779b" d="M17 5h11l-6 10z"/><path fill="#303b64" d="M31 5h11L29 28l-6-10z"/></svg><span>VOID <small>LANGUAGE</small></span></a><p>Native code. Clear intent.</p><div><a href="/docs/">Documentation</a><a href="https://github.com/Shmellyorc/Void.Language">GitHub ↗</a></div></div></footer><div class="sr-only" id="announcement" role="status"></div></body></html>
