-    <span class="keyword">return</span> boxes * <span class="number">3</span></code></pre><div class="guide-result" data-source="/docs/snippets/syntax.void" data-input="4" data-expected="12">Input <strong>4</strong><span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>Expected result <strong>12</strong></div></figure><section id="part-1"><h2>Read the function declaration</h2><p><code>func entry(boxes: i32) -&gt; i32:</code> names a function, its integer input and its integer result. Here a box holds three items, so four boxes produce <code>12</code> items.</p></section><section id="part-2"><h2>Use indentation for the body</h2><p>The colon starts the body; indent its statements by four spaces. <code>return boxes * 3</code> calculates and returns the item count. Ordinary blocks need no braces or statement-ending semicolons. A <code>#</code> comment explains the idea without affecting execution.</p></section><section id="part-3"><h2>Understand the entry adapter</h2><p>The current command-line adapter passes one <code>i32</code> input to <code>entry</code> and displays its returned integer. The first example uses that adapter; the applications below use a separate execution mode. Neither supplies a printing API. Use descriptive lowercase <code>snake_case</code> names.</p></section><section id="application"><h2>Choose an application entry point</h2><p>For application execution, use <code>voidc run main.void --app</code> with a zero-argument synchronous <code>main()</code>. A main without a result exits successfully; a main returning <code>i32</code> must return on every path. This short application deliberately returns status seven. That status belongs to the process and is <strong>not printed</strong>; standard output is empty. No numeric adapter input is passed.</p><figure class="guide-code"><figcaption>main.void<a href="/docs/snippets/application/main.void" download>Download source</a></figcaption><pre tabindex="0" aria-label="main.void source"><code data-source="/docs/snippets/application/main.void"><span class="keyword">func</span> main() -&gt; <span class="keyword">i32</span>:
-    <span class="keyword">return</span> <span class="number">7</span></code></pre><div class="guide-result" data-source="/docs/snippets/application/main.void" data-mode="app" data-expected-status="7" data-expected-stdout="">Exit status <strong>7</strong><span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>Standard output empty</div></figure><p>Executable top-level statements are an alternative to main, not something to combine with it. This source executes in order and returns success when capacity is available. Run its filename with <code>--app</code> too.</p><figure class="guide-code"><figcaption>top-level.void<a href="/docs/snippets/top-level.void" download>Download source</a></figcaption><pre tabindex="0" aria-label="top-level.void source"><code data-source="/docs/snippets/top-level.void"><span class="keyword">var</span> capacity = <span class="number">24</span>
+    <span class="keyword">return</span> boxes * <span class="number">3</span></code></pre><div class="guide-result" data-source="/docs/snippets/syntax.void" data-input="4" data-expected="12">Input <strong>4</strong><span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>Expected result <strong>12</strong></div></figure><section id="part-1"><h2>Read the function declaration</h2><p><code>func entry(boxes: i32) -&gt; i32:</code> names a function, its integer input and its integer result. Here a box holds three items, so four boxes produce <code>12</code> items.</p></section><section id="part-2"><h2>Use indentation for the body</h2><p>The colon starts the body; indent its statements by four spaces. <code>return boxes * 3</code> calculates and returns the item count. Ordinary blocks need no braces or statement-ending semicolons. A <code>#</code> comment explains the idea without affecting execution.</p></section><section id="part-3"><h2>Understand the entry adapter</h2><p>The current command-line adapter passes one <code>i32</code> input to <code>entry</code> and displays its returned integer. The first example uses that adapter; the compiler selects the application entry below from its checked source. Use <code>console.stdout</code> for explicit output. Use descriptive lowercase <code>snake_case</code> names.</p></section><section id="application"><h2>Choose an application entry point</h2><p>For application execution, use <code>voidc run main.void</code> with a zero-argument synchronous <code>main()</code>. A main without a result exits successfully; a main returning <code>i32</code> must return on every path. This short application deliberately returns status seven. That status belongs to the process and is <strong>not printed</strong>; standard output is empty. No numeric adapter input is passed.</p><figure class="guide-code"><figcaption>main.void<a href="/docs/snippets/application/main.void" download>Download source</a></figcaption><pre tabindex="0" aria-label="main.void source"><code data-source="/docs/snippets/application/main.void"><span class="keyword">func</span> main() -&gt; <span class="keyword">i32</span>:
+    <span class="keyword">return</span> <span class="number">7</span></code></pre><div class="guide-result" data-source="/docs/snippets/application/main.void" data-mode="app" data-expected-status="7" data-expected-stdout="">Exit status <strong>7</strong><span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>Standard output empty</div></figure><p>Executable top-level statements are an alternative to main, not something to combine with it. This source executes in order and returns success when capacity is available. Run it with <code>voidc run top-level.void</code>.</p><figure class="guide-code"><figcaption>top-level.void<a href="/docs/snippets/top-level.void" download>Download source</a></figcaption><pre tabindex="0" aria-label="top-level.void source"><code data-source="/docs/snippets/top-level.void"><span class="keyword">var</span> capacity = <span class="number">24</span>
 <span class="keyword">var</span> reserved = <span class="number">6</span>
 <span class="keyword">var</span> available = capacity - reserved
 <span class="keyword">return</span> <span class="keyword">if</span> available &gt; <span class="number">0</span>:
