+<main id="main" class="inner-page"><div class="container guide-layout"><aside class="guide-sidebar"><nav aria-label="Documentation topics"><a class="guide-overview" href="/docs/">All guides</a><details class="topic-group" open><summary>Foundations</summary><a href="/docs/syntax/">Syntax basics</a><a href="/docs/variables/">Variables & constants</a><a href="/docs/types/" aria-current="page">Types & numbers</a><a href="/docs/functions/">Functions</a><a href="/docs/control-flow/">Control flow</a><a href="/docs/modules/">Modules & imports</a></details><details class="topic-group"><summary>Data & behavior</summary><a href="/docs/collections/">Collections</a><a href="/docs/dictionaries/">Dictionaries</a><a href="/docs/strings/">Strings & Unicode</a><a href="/docs/structs/">Structs & memory</a><a href="/docs/initialization/">Initialization</a><a href="/docs/methods/">Receiver methods</a><a href="/docs/composition/">Composition</a><a href="/docs/inheritance/">Inheritance & dispatch</a><a href="/docs/interfaces/">Interfaces</a><a href="/docs/generics/">Generics</a><a href="/docs/properties/">Properties</a><a href="/docs/callables/">Callable structs</a><a href="/docs/closures/">Functions & closures</a><a href="/docs/events/">Events</a><a href="/docs/nullable/">Nullable values</a><a href="/docs/patterns/">Enums & pattern matching</a><a href="/docs/errors/">Results & errors</a><a href="/docs/cleanup/">Defer & resource cleanup</a></details><details class="topic-group"><summary>Concurrency</summary><a href="/docs/async/">Async & await</a><a href="/docs/task-groups/">Task groups & cancellation</a><a href="/docs/parallelism/">Parallel workers</a></details><details class="topic-group"><summary>Tooling & integration</summary><a href="/docs/native-integration/">Native integration</a><a href="/docs/editor-tooling/">Editor tooling & diagnostics</a><a href="/docs/live-development/">Live development</a></details><details class="topic-group"><summary>Library and applications</summary><a href="/docs/math-random/">Math and seeded random numbers</a><a href="/docs/collection-tools/">Collection helpers</a><a href="/docs/text-encoding/">Text and byte encodings</a><a href="/docs/binary-memory/">Binary values and memory streams</a><a href="/docs/files-paths/">Files and host paths</a><a href="/docs/os-directories/">Platform information and directories</a><a href="/docs/logging/">Logging</a><a href="/docs/channels/">Topic subscriptions</a><a href="/docs/url-http/">URLs and HTTP clients</a><a href="/docs/compiler-workflow/">Running and building applications</a><a href="/docs/console-output/">Writing to the console</a><a href="/docs/json-data/">Reading JSON data</a><a href="/docs/calendar-time/">Dates and clocks</a></details></nav></aside><article class="guide-article"><p class="eyebrow">LEARN VOID / 03</p><h1 class="page-title">Types & numbers</h1><p class="guide-tagline">Make the shape of data clear.</p><p>Complete program for the synchronous entry adapter. The result below is the returned integer.</p><figure class="guide-code"><figcaption>types.void<a href="/docs/snippets/types.void" download>Download source</a></figcaption><pre tabindex="0" aria-label="types.void source"><code data-source="/docs/snippets/types.void"><span class="keyword">func</span> entry(minutes: <span class="keyword">i32</span>) -&gt; <span class="keyword">i32</span>:
     <span class="keyword">var</span> seconds: <span class="keyword">i64</span> = (minutes <span class="keyword">as</span> <span class="keyword">i64</span>) * <span class="number">60</span>
     <span class="keyword">return</span> seconds <span class="keyword">as</span> <span class="keyword">i32</span></code></pre><div class="guide-result" data-source="/docs/snippets/types.void" data-input="5" data-expected="300">Input <strong>5</strong><span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>Expected result <strong>300</strong></div></figure><section id="part-1"><h2>Choose the numeric representation</h2><p>The input is minutes as <code>i32</code>; intermediate seconds use <code>i64</code>. Five minutes become <code>300</code> seconds. Signed integers are <code>i8</code>, <code>i16</code>, <code>i32</code> and <code>i64</code>; unsigned integers use <code>u8</code> through <code>u64</code>. Floating-point types are <code>f32</code> and <code>f64</code>.</p></section><section id="part-2"><h2>Convert explicitly</h2><p><code>minutes as i64</code> converts an already typed value. The literal <code>60</code> takes its contextual <code>i64</code> type. Returning through this CLI adapter requires <code>seconds as i32</code>. Conversions are checked: a value outside the target range raises a runtime failure. Integer arithmetic itself wraps.</p></section><section id="part-3"><h2>Keep conditions and text explicit</h2><p><code>bool</code> holds <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>. Conditions require Boolean values; logical expressions use <code>and</code>, <code>or</code> and <code>not</code>. UTF-8 text uses <code>string</code>. These types do not introduce implicit numeric or text conversions.</p></section><section id="numeric-prelude"><h2>Clamp values without an import</h2><p>Keep a volume in the inclusive range zero through one hundred. Input 120 returns 100, negative input returns zero and an in-range input stays unchanged.</p><figure class="guide-code"><figcaption>numeric-prelude.void<a href="/docs/snippets/numeric-prelude.void" download>Download source</a></figcaption><pre tabindex="0" aria-label="numeric-prelude.void source"><code data-source="/docs/snippets/numeric-prelude.void"><span class="keyword">func</span> entry(volume: <span class="keyword">i32</span>) -&gt; <span class="keyword">i32</span>:
-    <span class="keyword">return</span> volume.clamp(lower = <span class="number">0</span>, upper = <span class="number">100</span>)</code></pre><div class="guide-result" data-source="/docs/snippets/numeric-prelude.void" data-input="120" data-expected="100">Input <strong>120</strong><span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>Expected result <strong>100</strong></div></figure><p>All ten numeric types provide <code>T.min_value</code>, <code>T.max_value</code>, <code>T.abs(value)</code> and <code>T.clamp(value, lower, upper)</code>. Value forms such as <code>volume.abs()</code> and <code>volume.clamp(0, 100)</code> use the same operations. Signed-minimum absolute value and reversed clamp bounds fail with checked errors; NaN bounds also fail. Operations preserve the numeric type, without implicit widening. These are compiler-owned operations, available without a library import.</p></section><nav class="guide-pager" aria-label="Guide navigation"><a href="/docs/variables/">← Variables & constants</a><a href="/docs/functions/">Functions</a></nav></article><nav class="guide-toc" aria-label="On this page"><span>ON THIS PAGE</span><a href="#part-1">Choose the numeric representation</a><a href="#part-2">Convert explicitly</a><a href="#part-3">Keep conditions and text explicit</a><a href="#numeric-prelude">Clamp values without an import</a></nav></div></main><footer><div class="container footer-inner"><a class="brand" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 44 44" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M2 5h12l13 23-6 11z"/><path fill="#6b779b" d="M17 5h11l-6 10z"/><path fill="#303b64" d="M31 5h11L29 28l-6-10z"/></svg><span>VOID <small>LANGUAGE</small></span></a><p>Native code. Clear intent.</p><div><a href="/docs/">Documentation</a><a href="https://github.com/Shmellyorc/Void.Language">GitHub ↗</a></div></div></footer><div class="sr-only" id="announcement" role="status"></div></body></html>
