-<body><a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="header"><div class="container nav-wrap"><a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="VOID Language home"><svg viewBox="0 0 44 44" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M2 5h12l13 23-6 11z"/><path fill="#6b779b" d="M17 5h11l-6 10z"/><path fill="#303b64" d="M31 5h11L29 28l-6-10z"/></svg><span>VOID</span></a><button class="menu-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="navigation" hidden>Menu</button><nav id="navigation" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/">Home</a><a href="/why-void/">Why VOID</a><a href="/features/" aria-current="page">Features</a><a href="/examples/">Examples</a><a href="/docs/">Docs</a><a href="/getting-started/">Get started</a><a href="/roadmap/">Roadmap</a></nav><button class="theme-toggle" aria-label="Switch to dark theme" aria-pressed="false" hidden><svg class="sun-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="4"/><path d="M12 2v2m0 16v2M2 12h2m16 0h2M5 5l1.5 1.5m11 11L19 19M5 19l1.5-1.5m11-11L19 5"/></svg><svg class="moon-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M20.5 14a8.5 8.5 0 0 1-10.5-10.5A8.5 8.5 0 1 0 20.5 14Z"/></svg></button><a class="github-nav" href="https://github.com/Shmellyorc/Void.Language">GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></header>
-<main id="main" class="inner-page"><section id="features" class="section tinted"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">POWERFUL BY DESIGN</p><div class="section-heading"><h1 class="page-title">A thoughtful foundation.<br><span class="gradient">Real native execution.</span></h1><p>Readable source, useful types and native execution form VOID’s foundation. Explore what each feature means for your code and where development is heading.</p></div><h2 class="sr-only">VOID Language feature overview</h2><div class="features-grid"><article class="feature"><span class="feature-number">01</span><h3>Native compilation</h3><p>LLVM 19.1.7 powers both ORC JIT execution and ahead-of-time object output through one shared lowering pipeline.</p><a href="/features/#native">Explore native compilation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article><article class="feature"><span class="feature-number">02</span><h3>Managed memory</h3><p>A precise, non-moving tracing collector supports managed structs, nullable graphs and deep readonly access paths.</p><a href="/docs/structs/">Explore managed memory <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article><article class="feature"><span class="feature-number">03</span><h3>Expressive types</h3><p>Exact-width numbers, value structs, structural interfaces and concrete generics bring structure to everyday code.</p><a href="/docs/generics/">Explore expressive types <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article><article class="feature"><span class="feature-number">04</span><h3>Useful collections</h3><p>Lists, fixed arrays and dictionaries offer recoverable get operations; list pop handles empty collections explicitly.</p><a href="/docs/collections/">Explore useful collections <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article><article class="feature"><span class="feature-number">05</span><h3>Explicit control flow</h3><p>Named/default calls, typed if-expressions, string-only interpolation and explicit results keep everyday intent readable.</p><a href="/docs/errors/">Explore explicit control flow <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article><article class="feature"><span class="feature-number">06</span><h3>Bounded concurrency</h3><p>Cooperative async tasks and structured groups are implemented. Native workers currently accept a restricted value-only subset.</p><a href="/docs/async/">Explore bounded concurrency <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article></div></div></section><section class="section container feature-explanations"><article id="native"><span class="status implemented">Implemented foundation</span><h2>Native code, two ways to work.</h2><p>VOID compiles source through LLVM. Just-in-time execution runs compiled code during development; ahead-of-time compilation emits an object file that can be linked into a native executable.</p><p>Both paths use the same language rules and lowering pipeline. Applications can use a zero-argument main or executable top-level statements through explicit <code>--app</code> mode. <a href="/docs/syntax/#application">Application exit status</a> is separate from standard output. <a href="/docs/strings/">Unicode scalar indexing, scalar/byte counts and UTF-8 conversion</a> are implemented too. The compiler is built with C++20 and uses LLVM 19.1.7. VOID is still in development, with no public builds available yet.</p><a href="/docs/syntax/">See the shape of a VOID program</a></article><article id="generics"><span class="status implemented">Implemented foundation</span><h2>Write the idea once.</h2><p>Generic functions and structs express an operation or data shape in terms of a type parameter. A function such as <code>identity&lt;t&gt;(value: t) -&gt; t</code> can return its input while keeping its concrete type.</p><p>Concrete generic instantiations preserve types. Compile-time <code>Type has Target</code> checks direct using-components or structural interface conformance. The numeric prelude supplies limits, abs and clamp without imports. <a href="/docs/composition/#relationships">Explore type relationships</a> and <a href="/docs/types/#numeric-prelude">numeric operations</a>.</p><a href="/docs/generics/">Explore generics</a></article><article id="concurrency"><span class="status implemented">Implemented subset</span><h2>Express work that can wait.</h2><p>An <code>async func</code> can suspend at <code>await</code> while other cooperative tasks proceed. Structured task groups give related work a lifecycle, and bounded native workers support parallel execution of a restricted value-only subset.</p><p>These mechanisms have different roles. Cooperative async work is not automatically parallel, and worker tasks do not support arbitrary shared mutable objects. Completed execution frames can release their GC roots sooner, while result and task bookkeeping can still grow within a root execution. No constant-memory claim is made. General native-library loading and automatic hot-reload watching remain future work.</p><a href="/docs/async/">Explore async and await</a></article></section></main><footer><div class="container footer-inner"><a class="brand" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 44 44" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M2 5h12l13 23-6 11z"/><path fill="#6b779b" d="M17 5h11l-6 10z"/><path fill="#303b64" d="M31 5h11L29 28l-6-10z"/></svg><span>VOID <small>LANGUAGE</small></span></a><p>Native code. Clear intent.</p><div><a href="/docs/">Documentation</a><a href="https://github.com/Shmellyorc/Void.Language">GitHub ↗</a></div></div></footer><div class="sr-only" id="announcement" role="status"></div></body></html>
