-<main id="main" class="inner-page"><section class="section tinted"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">THE ROAD AHEAD</p><div class="section-heading"><h1 class="page-title">VOID Language roadmap.<br><span class="gradient">A clear direction.</span></h1><p>VOID is in active development. Here is what works, what is being improved and where the language is heading. No dates or completion percentages are promised.</p></div><div class="topic-jump"><a href="#foundation">Implemented foundation</a><a href="#current-development">Current development</a><a href="#future">Future direction</a></div></div></section><section class="section container feature-explanations" aria-label="Roadmap priorities"><article id="foundation"><span class="status implemented">Implemented foundation</span><h2>A working language and native runtime.</h2><p>The compiler uses C++20 and LLVM 19.1.7, with one shared JIT/AOT pipeline for native execution and object output. Static types, value structs, managed objects, collections, generics, structural interfaces, results and pattern matching are part of the working language. Named/default function and method arguments, a numeric prelude, scalar-aware text and UTF-8 conversion, recoverable collection operations, type relationship queries, typed if-expressions, multiline calls and string-only interpolation are implemented. <a href="/examples/#named-arguments">Explore recent examples</a>.</p><p>The runtime supports tracing garbage collection, deterministic resource cleanup, cooperative async/await, structured task groups and a restricted value-transfer subset for bounded native workers. Completed async frames can release their GC roots after execution retires; retained results and task bookkeeping still have broader lifetimes. This is not constant-memory async execution. These mechanisms have distinct lifetime and safety boundaries.</p><p>Trusted, statically linked native bindings and compatible function replacement exist within explicit host integration. General native-library loading and automatic save-triggered reload are separate future goals.</p><a href="/features/">Explore the implemented foundation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article><article id="current-development"><span class="status review">Current development</span><h2>Make everyday development better.</h2><p>Applications now support explicit <code>--app</code> execution with main or top-level statements, nearest-main discovery and nested project/standard-source import spelling. A shipped standard-library catalog is not implied. Current work focuses on broader platform validation, compatibility and dependable distribution.</p><p>The compiler-owned language server now implements completion, hover, signature help, definitions and references, document/workspace symbols, guarded rename and conservative formatting. VS Code, Neovim and Vim integrations have local Linux execution evidence; Vim rename remains disabled. VSCodium execution, new Windows/macOS editor combinations and clean-machine readiness remain unverified, and broader platform validation is ongoing. Fresh interactive-client checks for the newest tooling additions are still pending.</p><p>Local implementation and checks do not establish a public release or general platform certification. There are no public compiler or editor builds available here yet.</p><a href="/docs/editor-tooling/">Understand the developer experience <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article><article id="future"><span class="status planned">Future direction</span><h2>A broader ecosystem. A smoother flow.</h2><p>Longer-term goals include a useful standard library, package management and discovery, and broader native-library integration through typed VOID wrappers.</p><p>Automatic file watching and compatible save-triggered function replacement aim to improve live development without forcing restarts. A more complete editor experience and broader verified portability remain part of that direction. These goals are not claims of currently available packages, commands or releases.</p><a href="/why-void/">Read why VOID is being built <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article></section><section class="closing container"><div><p class="eyebrow">EXPLORE WHAT WORKS TODAY</p><h2>Start with <span class="gradient">the language.</span></h2></div><a class="button primary" href="/examples/">Explore VOID</a></section></main><footer><div class="container footer-inner"><a class="brand" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 44 44" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M2 5h12l13 23-6 11z"/><path fill="#6b779b" d="M17 5h11l-6 10z"/><path fill="#303b64" d="M31 5h11L29 28l-6-10z"/></svg><span>VOID <small>LANGUAGE</small></span></a><p>Native code. Clear intent.</p><div><a href="/docs/">Documentation</a><a href="https://github.com/Shmellyorc/Void.Language">GitHub ↗</a></div></div></footer><div class="sr-only" id="announcement" role="status"></div></body></html>
