 <main id="main" class="inner-page"><section class="section container why-manifesto"><p class="eyebrow">WHY VOID EXISTS</p><h1 class="why-title">Programming should feel<br><span class="gradient">like making something.</span></h1><p class="why-lead">Easy to use. Productive to work in. Fast where it matters. Fun to come back to.</p><div class="why-opening"><p>VOID starts with a practical frustration: the idea you want to build can be simpler than the machinery you have to manage to build it.</p><p>A small tool should take a small amount of code. A larger application should still have clear types, predictable behavior and room to grow. Working on native software should leave more attention for the program itself.</p></div><blockquote>The compiler and runtime should handle complexity so application developers do not have to.</blockquote><nav class="topic-jump" aria-label="Why VOID sections"><a href="#problems">The problems</a><a href="#balance">VOID’s approach</a><a href="#influences">The influences</a><a href="#direction">Where it is heading</a></nav></section>
 <section id="problems" class="section tinted"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">WHAT VOID IS TRYING TO IMPROVE</p><h2>Less friction between<br><span class="gradient">an idea and a working program.</span></h2><div class="why-problems">
-<article class="why-problem"><div><span class="small-label">01 / EVERYDAY CODE</span><h3>Keep simple things simple.</h3></div><div><p class="problem-statement">The friction: ceremony can make a small operation harder to read than the idea it expresses.</p><p>VOID uses indentation, compact declarations, inferred local types and direct construction. A function without a result omits the result annotation. Receiver methods name their receiver explicitly. One <code>for</code> keyword covers iteration, conditions and repetition. Named arguments, pure defaults and multiline calls let a call explain itself. Typed if-expressions choose a value directly; string interpolation accepts string expressions without hidden numeric conversions.</p><p>A small application can start with executable top-level statements or a zero-argument main, selected explicitly with <code>--app</code>. Recoverable collection lookups let missing data be ordinary control flow. The aim is a small, consistent set of rules that remains useful as the program grows. Powerful types and predictable behavior should still be there when you need them.</p><a href="/docs/syntax/">Explore the syntax</a></div></article>
+<article class="why-problem"><div><span class="small-label">01 / EVERYDAY CODE</span><h3>Keep simple things simple.</h3></div><div><p class="problem-statement">The friction: ceremony can make a small operation harder to read than the idea it expresses.</p><p>VOID uses indentation, compact declarations, inferred local types and direct construction. A function without a result omits the result annotation. Receiver methods name their receiver explicitly. One <code>for</code> keyword covers iteration, conditions and repetition. Named arguments, pure defaults and multiline calls let a call explain itself. Typed if-expressions choose a value directly; string interpolation accepts string expressions without hidden numeric conversions.</p><p>A small application can start with executable top-level statements or a zero-argument main, selected from the checked source. Recoverable collection lookups let missing data be ordinary control flow. The aim is a small, consistent set of rules that remains useful as the program grows. Powerful types and predictable behavior should still be there when you need them.</p><a href="/docs/syntax/">Explore the syntax</a></div></article>
 <article class="why-problem"><div><span class="small-label">02 / NATIVE SOFTWARE</span><h3>Bring readability to native execution.</h3></div><div><p class="problem-statement">The friction: a productive editing experience and control over native data should fit into the same workflow.</p><p>VOID brings readable source to a statically typed language built on LLVM. Exact-width numbers, value structs, managed objects, interfaces and generics give you ways to express the shape of your data and the behavior your code needs.</p><p>Performance work focuses on generated machine code, allocation behavior and collections measured with real applications. Native execution is a foundation to develop, not a substitute for measurement.</p><a href="/docs/types/">Explore types and data</a></div></article>
 <article class="why-problem"><div><span class="small-label">03 / MEMORY &amp; RESOURCES</span><h3>Keep lifetime machinery out of ordinary code.</h3></div><div><p class="problem-statement">The friction: memory bookkeeping competes with the work your application is supposed to do.</p><p>Automatic memory management is central to VOID. Ordinary code should not need manual frees, raw pointer arithmetic, an <code>unsafe</code> escape hatch, placement decisions or collector scheduling.</p><p>Values and shared objects still have distinct, explainable behavior. External resources have a separate lifetime: <code>with</code>, <code>defer</code> and <code>deinit</code> make deterministic release explicit rather than leaving it to garbage collection.</p><a href="/docs/structs/">Understand memory and identity</a></div></article>
 <article class="why-problem"><div><span class="small-label">04 / EXISTING LIBRARIES</span><h3>Make native integration feel like VOID.</h3></div><div><p class="problem-statement">The friction: using an existing library can expose foreign pointers, string formats and ownership rules throughout application code.</p><p>The direction is typed <code>.void</code> wrappers and trusted bindings that handle those boundaries. Application developers should work with language values and managed resource identities, rather than learn a second string type just to cross into C.</p><p><span class="inline-status">Implemented subset</span> Trusted host-provided static bindings demonstrate scalar calls, owned UTF-8 transfers, resources and callbacks. General library loading and a public binding workflow are still ahead.</p><a href="/docs/native-integration/">See the native integration boundary</a></div></article>
@@ -39,4 +39,4 @@
 <tr><th scope="row">GDScript</th><td>Clean object and property syntax, with an approachable editing experience.</td><td>Build an independent language that is not tied to a particular game engine.</td></tr>
 <tr><th scope="row">C#</th><td>Helpful editor feedback, useful pattern matching and strong diagnostics.</td><td>Pursue those qualities with a compact language surface and LLVM native execution, without a CLR dependency.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div></div></section>
-<section id="direction" class="section container why-direction"><p class="eyebrow">THE PROMISE TO WORK TOWARD</p><h2>Ambition with<br><span class="gradient">clear boundaries.</span></h2><p class="section-lead">VOID is in development. The direction is broad; every capability still has to earn its implementation.</p><div class="direction-grid"><article><h3>Working foundation</h3><p>Typed source, LLVM JIT/AOT, managed and value data, collections, generics, interfaces, patterns, closures, events, structured async work and bounded workers have implemented contracts.</p></article><article><h3>Still being built</h3><p>Automatic save-driven reload, broader native-library integration, package management and the complete public developer experience remain future work. There are no public builds available yet.</p></article></div><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="/docs/">Explore the language</a><a class="button secondary" href="/examples/">See it in code</a></div></section></main><footer><div class="container footer-inner"><a class="brand" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 44 44" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M2 5h12l13 23-6 11z"/><path fill="#6b779b" d="M17 5h11l-6 10z"/><path fill="#303b64" d="M31 5h11L29 28l-6-10z"/></svg><span>VOID <small>LANGUAGE</small></span></a><p>Native code. Clear intent.</p><div><a href="/docs/">Documentation</a><a href="https://github.com/Shmellyorc/Void.Language">GitHub ↗</a></div></div></footer><div class="sr-only" id="announcement" role="status"></div></body></html>
